ation, or a long curriculum. If energy, a tool, a file, or an error blocks the action, reduce the task again until a two-minute safe move is possible.\n11\t\n12\t## Make the movement executable\n13\t\n14\tDo not mistake a prerequisite for the first artifact. “Install the software,” “open the project,” “choose an animation,” or “watch this tutorial” only moves the ambiguity forward. Installation may be the first click, but the response must continue through the exact creative operation that follows it. Decide the application, existing asset or template, concrete content, output name, stopping point, and visible proof for the user. Verify current paths, labels, and shortcuts against primary documentation when a changing interface matters.\n15\t\n16\tApply the tired-user test: the user should be able to execute without choosing the software, project, asset, subject, movement, duration, or meaning of “done.” Make those small reversible decisions on their behalf unless they would change the larger creative direction. For character animation, specify the character or rig, one named movement, its duration or frame range, the key poses with timings, the project or sequence filename, and the capture to produce. Prefer a bounded technical spike inside the user's intended production pipeline over an isolated preview that teaches no transferable gesture. The user must manipulate at least one meaningful control; merely watching a stock animation is consumption, not a first movement.\n17\t\n18\t## The high-energy projection gap\n19\t\n20\tWhen ambition appears during sport, travel, a walk, or another activated state but collapses on returning home, do not diagnose a lack of desire or discipline. The user may be enjoying a vivid, cost-free projection while temporarily separated from the concrete friction of the software and the work; the transition home then adds fatigue, hunger, showering, choices, and immediate-reward alternatives. A large meal can deepen the drop through relaxa